# Manifest build profiles for scripts/build-manifest.mjs
#
# manifest.template.xml is the source of truth. manifest.xml is generated from
# it and is gitignored, so never hand-edit manifest.xml.
#
# For local development you need none of this:
#
#   npm run manifest:dev
#
# That writes manifest.xml pointing at https://localhost:3000 using the
# script's built-in defaults, which is what `npm start` sideloads.
#
# The variables below override those defaults to emit a manifest for a hosted
# build. Copy the block, fill in real values, and run:
#
#   EXCELENTE_BASE_URL=... EXCELENTE_ADDIN_ID=... EXCELENTE_DISPLAY_NAME=... \
#     npm run manifest:prod        # writes dist/manifest.xml

# --- Your hosted build ---
# EXCELENTE_BASE_URL=https://excelente.example.com
# EXCELENTE_ADDIN_ID=<generate a fresh GUID>
# EXCELENTE_DISPLAY_NAME=Your Add-in Name

# Notes:
#
# - BASE_URL is the origin serving taskpane.html, commands.html, and /assets/*.
#   A trailing slash is stripped for you.
#
# - ADDIN_ID must be a GUID that is yours. Office keys the installed add-in on
#   this id, so two builds sharing one cannot be installed side by side, and
#   reusing someone else's id will collide with their installation. Generate
#   one with `node -e "console.log(crypto.randomUUID())"`.
#
# - If you run more than one instance (say a staging build and a production
#   build) give each its own GUID and its own display name so you can tell them
#   apart in Excel and install both at once.
#
# - Store submissions carry their own privacy policy and support URLs in the
#   store listing metadata, not in the manifest XML.
